# The dependency audit's RESIDUAL (issue #961, ADR 0008).
#
# Every line here is an advisory this repository knows about and has decided not
# to act on yet. The ledger exists because ADR 0008 requires that "suppressions
# are visible or they do not happen": a disabled check is invisible, a filed
# issue is not. `scripts/dependency-audit.py` prints the count of suppressed and
# deferred findings on EVERY run, including the clean one, so a green from this
# gate never reads as "there is nothing here".
#
# A line that accounts for NOTHING is a RED (`DEP-AUDIT-STALE:`), not a quiet
# no-op. So when the issue named on a line is fixed, this gate fails until the
# line comes out, and the residual cannot outlive the thing it records. Nothing
# here stops the list GROWING - that side is not solved, only named.
#
#   advisory <path> <package> <version> <advisory-id> <issue>
#       ONE finding, pinned to the exact version. A version bump or a NEW
#       advisory on the same package does not match and is reported.
#
#   deferred <path> <issue>
#       every finding in ONE file, while a filed issue owns that file's whole
#       dependency set. Scoped to the named path and nothing else.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# Root lock - #922: "tracked by no issue" until someone looked. This gate is the
# standing check that was missing; remediating the two findings stays with #922,
# which is the scope line issue #961 draws.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
advisory uv.lock pygments 2.19.2 PYSEC-2026-2987 #922
advisory uv.lock pytest 9.0.2 PYSEC-2026-1845 #922

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
# mcp-evaluate - #943 owns this project's whole dependency set: 32 advisories
# across 10 packages, measured 2026-09-16. Deferred rather than enumerated
# because a gate that blocks every pull request in the repository on day one is
# a gate that gets switched off, and switching it off is itself an oscillation
# (ADR 0009). The findings are NOT hidden: they are counted and printed on every
# run beside this issue number.
#
# WHAT WOULD MOVE THIS BACK (ADR 0009): #943 landing, or mcp-evaluate's residual
# falling to a handful. Either makes per-advisory lines affordable, and this
# blanket should then be replaced by them rather than inherited. The gate forces
# the conversation on its own: once this file reports nothing, the deferral is
# STALE and reddens until it is removed.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------- #
deferred mcp-evaluate/uv.lock #943
